WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:05.620
Rate limits are one of the first reasons a workflow that works in a test run fails in production.

2
00:00:05.720 --> 00:00:10.120
Ten items may complete cleanly because the external service never feels pressure.

3
00:00:10.220 --> 00:00:18.620
Several thousand items can trigger quota limits, temporary throttling, or HTTP 429 responses that indicate too many requests.

4
00:00:18.720 --> 00:00:25.600
Reliable loop engineering treats rate limits as part of the design instead of as an error message to handle after the workflow breaks.

5
00:00:25.880 --> 00:00:27.800
Several controls work together.

6
00:00:27.900 --> 00:00:31.980
Batch size determines how many items are released in one loop iteration.

7
00:00:32.080 --> 00:00:36.800
The number of operations depends on how many downstream calls each item causes.

8
00:00:36.900 --> 00:00:41.780
A delay between iterations reduces sustained pressure on the external API.

9
00:00:41.880 --> 00:00:45.800
Retry behavior controls what happens when a specific request fails.

10
00:00:45.900 --> 00:00:50.140
Execution duration increases as pacing becomes more conservative.

11
00:00:50.240 --> 00:00:55.040
Idempotency determines whether repeating a failed operation is safe.

12
00:00:55.320 --> 00:00:59.080
The official Loop Over Items plus Wait pattern is straightforward.

13
00:00:59.180 --> 00:01:07.180
Place the API operation inside the loop body, then place a Wait node after the operation and before the return connection to Loop Over Items.

14
00:01:07.280 --> 00:01:08.800
That position matters.

15
00:01:08.900 --> 00:01:15.460
The Wait node pauses the workflow after the current batch has completed its API call and before the next batch is released.

16
00:01:15.560 --> 00:01:20.200
It is deliberate pacing between iterations, not a retry by itself.

17
00:01:20.480 --> 00:01:24.800
The HTTP Request node also has native batching capabilities.

18
00:01:24.900 --> 00:01:31.140
When a single HTTP Request node can satisfy the requirement, native batching may be the simplest option.

19
00:01:31.240 --> 00:01:37.800
Settings such as items per batch and batch interval can pace requests without adding an explicit multi-node loop.

20
00:01:37.900 --> 00:01:51.260
Loop Over Items becomes more useful when the unit of work is an entire sequence: request data, transform it, branch on a result, write to a destination, log status, and only then release the next batch.

21
00:01:51.540 --> 00:01:54.020
Retry on Fail solves a different problem.

22
00:01:54.120 --> 00:02:03.080
It is meant for transient failures, such as a temporary network problem, a short service interruption, or a 429 response that may succeed after a delay.

23
00:02:03.180 --> 00:02:06.700
Max Tries sets a ceiling on how many attempts the node should make.

24
00:02:06.800 --> 00:02:10.160
Wait Between Tries sets the pause between those attempts.

25
00:02:10.260 --> 00:02:16.020
That retry delay is not the same as the normal pacing delay between successful loop iterations.

26
00:02:16.120 --> 00:02:19.720
A workflow can need both, but they answer different questions.

27
00:02:20.000 --> 00:02:22.640
Side effects change the risk profile.

28
00:02:22.740 --> 00:02:32.820
Retrying a read operation is usually different from retrying a payment, message send, record creation, ticket update, or any other write that affects an external system.

29
00:02:32.920 --> 00:02:36.920
If the destination supports idempotency keys, use them.

30
00:02:37.020 --> 00:02:42.860
If it supports upserts based on a stable external identifier, prefer that over blind creation.

31
00:02:42.960 --> 00:02:51.760
If neither is available, add destination checks or status fields that prevent duplicate effects when a retry occurs after an ambiguous failure.

32
00:02:52.040 --> 00:02:55.800
The safest design records what happened at the item level.

33
00:02:55.900 --> 00:02:59.260
A processed status means the intended operation completed.

34
00:02:59.360 --> 00:03:03.600
A skipped status means the workflow deliberately did not call the destination.

35
00:03:03.700 --> 00:03:08.180
A failed status means the item reached a controlled failure path.

36
00:03:08.280 --> 00:03:11.640
A retried count shows how much recovery work was needed.

37
00:03:11.740 --> 00:03:19.420
Those fields make a paced loop auditable, especially when an external service throttles some requests while accepting others.

38
00:03:19.700 --> 00:03:23.380
Rate-limit tuning should also consider the total runtime of the job.

39
00:03:23.480 --> 00:03:29.400
A delay that is perfect for one hundred records may be too slow for a nightly job with fifty thousand records.

40
00:03:29.500 --> 00:03:34.940
A batch size that is safe during office hours may need to be smaller when the external service is under load.

41
00:03:35.040 --> 00:03:44.240
The workflow should begin with conservative defaults and adjust based on measured response codes, retry frequency, destination results, and operational windows.

42
00:03:44.520 --> 00:03:47.480
The design should also make throttling visible.

43
00:03:47.580 --> 00:03:55.980
Record when a request was retried, when pacing delayed the next batch, and when a final status came from a clean response rather than a recovered attempt.

44
00:03:56.080 --> 00:04:00.320
That history helps separate API capacity problems from workflow logic defects.

45
00:04:00.600 --> 00:04:03.800
Choosing an initial batch size should be conservative.

46
00:04:03.900 --> 00:04:08.860
Start with a small batch and a delay that respects the published or observed API limit.

47
00:04:08.960 --> 00:04:15.440
Then inspect execution duration, response codes, retry counts, and destination results.

48
00:04:15.540 --> 00:04:21.300
Increase throughput gradually only when the evidence shows that the external service and the workflow can handle it.

49
00:04:21.400 --> 00:04:28.120
A fast workflow that creates duplicate records or fails halfway through a large job is not reliable.

50
00:04:28.220 --> 00:04:34.140
A paced workflow that produces traceable results is usually easier to operate and recover.
